{
 "what": "The head of the map of each struct in the schema form, carsales, 20000 parking lots. Arm A: main c2349d3, the head in preferred serialization (1 byte up to 23 members). Arm B: the head always with a 2-byte argument (0xb9). Arm C: always with a 4-byte argument (0xba). Encode into a kept std::string and cbor::decode<parking_lot>, ns per car; bytes per car.",
 "why": "A fixed width for the number of members keeps the offsets of a struct when members are added. The owner asked how much 2 and 4 bytes cost.",
 "how": "Google Benchmark, one binary per arm, each built twice (a, b), 3 rounds alternating, --benchmark_min_time=0.1s --benchmark_repetitions=3, random interleaving, GLIBC_TUNABLES trim_threshold, mmap_threshold and top_pad high. The whole run was made twice.",
 "finding": "Bytes per car: A 467.8, B 479.8 (+2.6 percent), C 491.8 (+5.1 percent); a car holds 6 structs (car, engine, 4 wheels). Time, B against A and C against A, run 1 / run 2: encode +2.5 / +3.6 and -0.3 / +1.4 percent, decode +3.4 / +1.9 and +3.8 / +0.9 percent, with a noise of up to 3.9 percent in run 1 and 2.1 percent in run 2. C is not slower than B. Only the size differs beyond doubt.",
